He could not save his mother from the marauders. He spent hours wandering the wasteland, aimlessly looking for his final resting place. He finally sat down on the dusty, dry ground in the shade of some tall rocks, his look gazing at his final sunset. He must have sat there for over an hour, shifting his look from the sun to his father’s pistol. It did not save his mother, but it did save him, and that must count for something. His life was already gone, what difference did it make if he ended it all here or in some desperate attempt to redeem his failure while saving another life? As he dwelled on these thoughts, he felt a thirst return to his throat, and his stomach alerted him that he would in fact, not mind a dinner.


She was known amongst her colleagues for rarely using her gun. Despite this, she practiced her aim more than anyone. Her work in the Etergarde had increasingly been forcing her into hostile confrontations, and her methods of dialogue and empathy sometimes just didn’t work. When she did draw her weapon, she had to be sure that the shot would not kill. Those few times she failed she would scratch another line into both her implement and her arm. The list would serve as a reminder of how many drinks she would need to order whenever she started to hear their bloodied gurgles and pained screams again.


He had four belts lined with pistols, two rifles on his shoulders and a whole arsenal of bombs. He was not scared of any kind of company or gang - he had enough gunpowder for them all. He left scorched earth and graveyards of slavers everywhere he passed. As it tends to go, he would meet his end in one of his massive shootouts, peppered by bullets and finally stabbed and beaten to death by some armed adolescents. In his last few breaths he thought back to where he made the mistake, reliving his final standoff. It was not there. He thought further back, searched far and wide for the cause of his death. He didn’t have time to finish his thesis, but in this oblivious state it occurred to him that no matter how many of them he killed, more would rise up to take their place. With hundreds of souls to his name and fear in his heart, he considered if he did the right thing.
________________
Gunlingers are outlaws and vigilantes, those who wander the world in search of a purpose with a firearm at their side. They see their guns as the great equalizers that they are and use them to attempt to change the world through “civilized” violence. Whether they do this by a single, devastating headshot or a barrage of fire, you can be sure to expect them not to miss their mark. Most gunslingers die as they lived – alone, full of energy and pondering all of the things they still didn’t change.
________________
Recommended disciplines: Precision
Recommended specializations: Scout, Interface, Engineering, Pilot


