When her mother disappeared she tried her best to make it work. She offered herself for all sorts of work, yet most would reject her for her nearly skeletal appearance. How was she to feed her baby brother without money? A loaf of bread stolen here, some jewelry snatched in a busy street there – sometimes she felt like she could really make it work. Other times they’d go a week without food, becoming more jaded with the indifference of the world, becoming more open to depraved acts. She went over this in her head a thousand times before torching those fields, consulting her heart. She would take what was rightfully hers, they left her no other choice.


The newspaper spoke of a legendary marauder being finally put in his place by the heroic militias. He was in the business of offering “protection” to hamlets and villages, aided by his countless demonstrations of why protection was needed. The tyrant ruled the region with an iron fist for a decade, and nobody could or bothered to muster enough firepower to repel his minions. The twist lay in the fact that he had not perished due to the heroism of small people, but a personal and deeply uncharacteristic blunder. In his final year, he had fallen madly in love with a local woman, going as far as to conceive a child. He would cut corners from his vast empire, all to fund a majestic homestead for his new family. After months of infiltrating this undersupplied network, the militias had finally caught wind of the location of his family home. The article concluded by jubilantly describing the remnants of that tragic scene, ending it by mockingly referring to the deceased as being “killed by sentimentality”.


She wiped the fresh blood from her blade. This night's work had been the final point in her grand plan, and with a little luck, this may very well have been her last kill. The six of them hadn’t even noticed she snuck into the building, and with her swift movements she made sure to both secure her position at the top of the city’s underground and to eliminate the final person who could identify her. The moon was full that night, and a slight breeze signaled that it was the perfect time to enjoy the sights of the grand metropolis. She climbed one of the ruined towers, making a final mental check, confirming she was materially set for life. Reaching the top she made herself comfortable on one of the overgrown edges of the railing, picking up a white flower that grew there. She smelled it and looked over her city, content, happy for the first time since she could remember. Gazing over the families enjoying the evening she felt her heart skip a beat. Spotting some couples out on a walk made her suddenly feel cold. She quickly shook it off and went back down, assuring herself that ghosts are best left unseen.
________________


Rogues are chaotic and resourceful operatives and swords for hire. They excel in ambushes and other underhanded tactics, preferring to make their own luck instead of wasting time on concepts like training and discipline. Distractions are a rogue’s favorite weapon, be they loud and explosive or more subtle and elegant. Rogues come from various molds, ranging from simple highwaymen and bandits to eloquent members of high society.
________________
