CH VIII. Items and Downtime
To finally complete the bigger picture of a character, we should take a look at the equipment they have access to, tools they can utilize and resources they require to stay alive. This chapter also covers the mechanics of ambedic constructs and mechs, as well as explaining how equipment can be augmented by the characters and how specific crafting systems work.


Reading item effects
Items often have specific requirements for effective use and effects they apply when used in their default way (e.g. attacking with a weapon).


Requirements detail how many hands are required to wield a weapon as well as a required attribute investment. Failing to meet the requirements prevents the user from using the weapon for discipline abilities.
                        * [ 1H GRT ] → This is a one-handed weapon that requires at least a point in GRT to use effectively.
                        * [ 2H REF|INT ] →  This is a two-handed weapon that requires at least a point in reflex or intelligence to use effectively.
                        * [ 1H/2H GRT ] → This is a weapon that requires at least a point in GRT to use effectively and can be used in either one or two hands.


Damage is displayed for all possible modes of use:
                        * [ 3 | 1 ] → This weapon deals 3 DMG in melee and 1 DMG for ranged attacks.
                        * [ ⅘ | 0 ] → This weapon deals 4 DMG when used in melee and one hand or 5 DMG when used in melee and two hands. It deals no DMG at range.


When keywords are used in effects, it is important to note the distinction between these two cases:
                        * [ Break 1 ] → This weapon always inflicts break 1 before other modifiers
                        * [ +1 Break] → The break this weapon would inflict is increased by +1


Certain weapons have extended ranges which are for the sake of simplicity written like this:
                        * [ A ] → This weapon’s optimal range is Adjacent
                        * [ A+1 ] → This weapon’s optimal range is Adjacent increased by 1 tile
________________
Weapons
Weapons in Reverie are split between a wide range of categories, each with multiple specific weapons within. On their own, these categories do not share or grant any stats or bonuses but they do determine which abilities the weapons can perform, as listed in each ability’s “requires” section. If there isn’t one, it’s safe to assume the ability can be performed with any equipment.


The 13 categories of weapons are:
                        * Sword - versatile sidearms and large horde weapons
                        * Axe - destructive and bloody single target weapons and tools
                        * Blunt - heavy and armour breaking weapons
                        * Polearm - long range and group combat weapons
                        * Bow - bows and crossbows of varying sizes
                        * Stealth - discrete blades and delivery mechanisms
                        * Pistol - single handed firearms
                        * Rifle - large battlefield firearms
                        * Eidoloscope - memory interfacing trinkets and prosthetics
                        * Leech - hemomantic parasitic leeches
                        * Instrument - acoustic and ambedic music channelers
                        * Focus - season channeling trinkets and symbols
                        * Unconventional - various tools repurposed for combat
